1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,350
A drone slips toward a power substation at dusk.

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:04,622
No radar sees it.

3
00:00:04,622 --> 00:00:06,197
No camera sees it yet.

4
00:00:06,197 --> 00:00:09,771
But the six-G base station beside the fence does, using

5
00:00:09,771 --> 00:00:12,860
nothing but the signal it already uses to talk.

6
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,920
Critical sites have a blind spot:

7
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:17,691
small, low-flying drones.

8
00:00:17,691 --> 00:00:19,706
Six-G proposes something new.

9
00:00:19,706 --> 00:00:24,958
Integrated sensing and communication, where the network itself becomes the sensor.

10
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:28,012
It is not standardized yet, so we simulate it,

11
00:00:28,012 --> 00:00:31,920
because real hardware tests are costly or simply impossible.

12
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,530
Here is how it is built.

13
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:38,278
A ray-tracing model of the substation runs at thirteen

14
00:00:38,278 --> 00:00:42,156
gigahertz, with a four-hundred-megahertz O F D M signal.

15
00:00:42,156 --> 00:00:46,744
Sionna computes the clutter from buildings, transformers and fence.

16
00:00:46,744 --> 00:00:49,523
The drone is a moving point target of a

17
00:00:49,523 --> 00:00:53,659
hundred square centimetres, flying twelve metres per second.

18
00:00:53,659 --> 00:00:57,020
The whole simulation runs on one AWS L four GPU.

19
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:59,313
Here is the sequence.

20
00:00:59,313 --> 00:01:02,717
The base station's echo goes to the sensing pipeline.

21
00:01:02,717 --> 00:01:04,688
Detections go to the tracker.

22
00:01:04,688 --> 00:01:08,092
A confirmed track goes to the evidence tools, and the

23
00:01:08,092 --> 00:01:11,615
evidence, the rules and the action catalog go to Llama.

24
00:01:11,615 --> 00:01:14,183
Its proposal goes to the policy engine,

25
00:01:14,183 --> 00:01:16,154
then to a human for approval.

26
00:01:16,154 --> 00:01:19,200
Every message is also written to the audit log.

27
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:20,889
The numbers.

28
00:01:20,889 --> 00:01:23,834
At thirteen gigahertz, in the upper mid band that

29
00:01:23,834 --> 00:01:26,890
six-G is expected to use, four hundred megahertz of

30
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:30,946
bandwidth gives a range resolution of about thirty-eight centimetres.

31
00:01:30,946 --> 00:01:33,946
One thousand twenty-four subcarriers set a maximum

32
00:01:33,946 --> 00:01:36,502
range of three hundred eighty-four metres.

33
00:01:36,502 --> 00:01:39,557
A frame of two thousand forty-eight symbols gives a

34
00:01:39,557 --> 00:01:42,780
speed resolution of one point eight metres per second.

35
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:46,717
The scene is a substation modelled in Sionna:

36
00:01:46,717 --> 00:01:51,261
concrete buildings, steel transformers, gantries and a metal fence.

37
00:01:51,261 --> 00:01:54,526
Ray tracing finds fifteen strong clutter paths.

38
00:01:54,526 --> 00:01:57,663
They are huge compared to the drone, but they

39
00:01:57,663 --> 00:02:01,055
do not move, so subtracting the average over time

40
00:02:01,055 --> 00:02:04,640
removes them, and what remains is the moving target.

41
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:10,097
The base station removes the static clutter and forms a range-Doppler map.

42
00:02:10,097 --> 00:02:13,321
A C F A R detector flags anything above the noise.

43
00:02:13,321 --> 00:02:16,725
A sixteen by eight antenna panel estimates direction,

44
00:02:16,725 --> 00:02:19,292
and a Kalman tracker follows the drone.

45
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:22,516
The track is confirmed at three hundred twenty-one

46
00:02:22,516 --> 00:02:25,860
metres, about twenty-seven seconds before the fence.

47
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:28,166
The detector is short.

48
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:31,410
A box filter estimates the local noise from training

49
00:02:31,410 --> 00:02:34,596
cells, leaving out the guard cells, and a threshold

50
00:02:34,596 --> 00:02:37,956
factor derived from the false-alarm target is applied.

51
00:02:37,956 --> 00:02:41,200
It runs on the GPU over two million cells per frame.

52
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,456
Direction comes from a sixteen by eight antenna panel.

53
00:02:45,456 --> 00:02:48,375
The drone must be seen in three frames before

54
00:02:48,375 --> 00:02:51,891
the track is confirmed, which filters out false alarms.

55
00:02:51,891 --> 00:02:55,823
A Kalman filter then predicts and corrects position and speed.

56
00:02:55,823 --> 00:02:59,100
The error keeps shrinking as the drone gets closer.

57
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,814
Across twenty simulated flights, position error is

58
00:03:02,814 --> 00:03:05,135
sixty-four centimetres R M S, and speed

59
00:03:05,135 --> 00:03:07,294
error is under one metre per second.

60
00:03:07,294 --> 00:03:10,964
A target ten times smaller is still confirmed at the same range.

61
00:03:10,964 --> 00:03:14,418
At three hundred metres, the smallest target detected ninety

62
00:03:14,418 --> 00:03:17,440
percent of the time is about ten square centimetres.

63
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:19,691
Target size matters.

64
00:03:19,691 --> 00:03:23,259
The smallest target detected ninety percent of the time

65
00:03:23,259 --> 00:03:26,827
at three hundred metres is about six square centimetres

66
00:03:26,827 --> 00:03:29,971
at a false-alarm setting of one in ten thousand,

67
00:03:29,971 --> 00:03:33,055
and ten square centimetres at one in a million.

68
00:03:33,055 --> 00:03:35,836
Birds in the same size range would also be

69
00:03:35,836 --> 00:03:38,739
detected, so telling them apart is a job for

70
00:03:38,739 --> 00:03:41,460
the agent and its rules, and a next step.

71
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:44,090
Accuracy depends on range.

72
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,322
At three hundred metres the median error is about

73
00:03:47,322 --> 00:03:50,554
one metre for the standard drone, and about three

74
00:03:50,554 --> 00:03:53,116
metres for a target ten times smaller.

75
00:03:53,116 --> 00:03:57,080
Inside one hundred metres, both are under twenty centimetres.

76
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:59,521
Now the governed agent.

77
00:03:59,521 --> 00:04:04,019
When the track is confirmed, deterministic tools compute the evidence:

78
00:04:04,019 --> 00:04:08,821
distance, zone, time to the fence, and whether a registered flight matches.

79
00:04:08,821 --> 00:04:13,441
Then Meta's Llama three point one proposes actions from a fixed catalog.

80
00:04:13,441 --> 00:04:14,900
It never acts alone.

81
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:18,317
These are the real configuration files.

82
00:04:18,317 --> 00:04:22,944
The policy file defines the zones, the actions, and which need approval.

83
00:04:22,944 --> 00:04:26,535
The radio settings fix thirteen gigahertz, four hundred

84
00:04:26,535 --> 00:04:29,761
megahertz and a hundred square centimetre target.

85
00:04:29,761 --> 00:04:32,379
Deployment is one AWS L four box, Llama

86
00:04:32,379 --> 00:04:35,179
three point one at temperature zero with a

87
00:04:35,179 --> 00:04:38,040
fixed seed, and a static page behind Caddy.

88
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:41,260
A policy engine checks every proposal.

89
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:43,796
It raises the threat level to the policy

90
00:04:43,796 --> 00:04:46,678
floor, adds the minimum actions for each zone,

91
00:04:46,678 --> 00:04:49,213
and blocks anything outside the catalog.

92
00:04:49,213 --> 00:04:51,058
Jamming is never executable.

93
00:04:51,058 --> 00:04:54,458
Actions that touch the physical world wait for a human.

94
00:04:54,458 --> 00:04:58,780
The duty officer approves the camera in three seconds, then the patrol.

95
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:02,359
And the workflow for each proposed action.

96
00:05:02,359 --> 00:05:04,171
The policy engine decides.

97
00:05:04,171 --> 00:05:07,131
Internal, reversible steps run automatically.

98
00:05:07,131 --> 00:05:10,575
Jamming, or anything outside the catalog, is blocked.

99
00:05:10,575 --> 00:05:13,716
Everything else waits for a human, who approves,

100
00:05:13,716 --> 00:05:16,858
rejects, or lets it expire after twenty seconds.

101
00:05:16,858 --> 00:05:20,120
Only approved actions run, and only in simulation.

102
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:25,156
The security manager rejects an early police escalation, and approves

103
00:05:25,156 --> 00:05:27,767
it only when the drone is over the fence.

104
00:05:27,767 --> 00:05:30,900
The dashboard lets you play the operator yourself.

105
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:33,917
The model is useful, but not trusted.

106
00:05:33,917 --> 00:05:36,674
In one run it described the drone as inside the

107
00:05:36,674 --> 00:05:39,863
restricted zone while it was still in the warning zone.

108
00:05:39,863 --> 00:05:43,539
A grounding check caught the mismatch and flagged it in the log.

109
00:05:43,539 --> 00:05:45,864
In another, the model was slow to alert

110
00:05:45,864 --> 00:05:48,892
the airspace authority, so the policy now guarantees

111
00:05:48,892 --> 00:05:51,000
that action in the restricted zone.

112
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:54,135
This is the policy engine in code.

113
00:05:54,135 --> 00:05:56,777
If the drone is unregistered and in the

114
00:05:56,777 --> 00:05:59,849
restricted zone, the threat level is raised to

115
00:05:59,849 --> 00:06:02,491
at least high, whatever the model said.

116
00:06:02,491 --> 00:06:06,300
Actions outside the catalog, such as jamming, are blocked.

117
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:10,299
Every step goes into a hash-chained audit log.

118
00:06:10,299 --> 00:06:13,049
Change one entry, and the chain breaks.

119
00:06:13,049 --> 00:06:16,120
You can verify it yourself, in your browser.

120
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:18,263
And the audit log.

121
00:06:18,263 --> 00:06:21,498
Each entry stores the hash of the previous entry,

122
00:06:21,498 --> 00:06:24,672
so changing any line breaks every hash after it.

123
00:06:24,672 --> 00:06:28,457
The dashboard recomputes these hashes in your browser, and

124
00:06:28,457 --> 00:06:32,180
the self-test confirms that a tampered entry is detected.

125
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:33,969
Honest limits.

126
00:06:33,969 --> 00:06:37,488
At closest approach the drone moves sideways, falls into the

127
00:06:37,488 --> 00:06:40,786
Doppler blind zone, and the track coasts for two frames.

128
00:06:40,786 --> 00:06:44,304
A hovering drone would be invisible to this clutter removal.

129
00:06:44,304 --> 00:06:46,777
And human approval delay eats most of the

130
00:06:46,777 --> 00:06:49,086
lead time late in the flight, which is

131
00:06:49,086 --> 00:06:51,120
why the agent must propose early.

132
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:55,780
Everything is in version control, one commit per stage, from

133
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:59,158
the radio simulation to the dashboard and this film.

134
00:06:59,158 --> 00:07:03,018
The code is in a private repository, with access on request.

135
00:07:03,018 --> 00:07:07,059
The self-test passes all six checks, and replaying the recorded

136
00:07:07,059 --> 00:07:10,980
Llama answers reproduces the same event sequence with no GPU.

137
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:14,146
The stack is deliberately simple.

138
00:07:14,146 --> 00:07:18,111
Sionna and PyTorch simulate the radio on a single AWS GPU.

139
00:07:18,111 --> 00:07:22,715
Meta's Llama three point one, eight billion parameters, runs locally

140
00:07:22,715 --> 00:07:26,295
through Ollama, so no incident data leaves the site.

141
00:07:26,295 --> 00:07:29,621
A policy engine and an audit log wrap the model.

142
00:07:29,621 --> 00:07:32,178
Two simplifications, stated plainly:

143
00:07:32,178 --> 00:07:34,480
the drone is a point target, and

144
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:37,102
the angle estimate is modelled at the

145
00:07:37,102 --> 00:07:40,108
detected cell, not a full array simulation.

146
00:07:40,108 --> 00:07:44,200
The public demo is a static page that replays recorded runs.

147
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,173
Why it matters.

148
00:07:46,173 --> 00:07:49,261
Substations, ports and airports are regulated.

149
00:07:49,261 --> 00:07:51,854
They answer to aviation rules, such as

150
00:07:51,854 --> 00:07:54,015
Remote I D, and to privacy law,

151
00:07:54,015 --> 00:07:56,855
because sensing data can be personal data.

152
00:07:56,855 --> 00:08:00,190
A system that proposes, a human that approves, and

153
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:02,907
a log that proves what happened is how A

154
00:08:02,907 --> 00:08:05,500
I earns a place in these environments.

155
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:07,314
What is next.

156
00:08:07,314 --> 00:08:10,118
First, validate the signal processing on an

157
00:08:10,118 --> 00:08:12,982
open-source five-G testbed with real radios.

158
00:08:12,982 --> 00:08:16,144
Second, add rotor signatures, so a hovering drone

159
00:08:16,144 --> 00:08:18,113
and a bird can be told apart.

160
00:08:18,113 --> 00:08:20,917
Third, fuse several base stations to remove

161
00:08:20,917 --> 00:08:23,244
the blind zone at closest approach.

162
00:08:23,244 --> 00:08:27,838
Fourth, replace the modelled angle estimate with a full array simulation.

163
00:08:27,838 --> 00:08:30,403
Six-G sensing is still being studied in

164
00:08:30,403 --> 00:08:32,432
the standard, so this is a map

165
00:08:32,432 --> 00:08:34,520
of what to test, not a product.

166
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:40,731
Governed, auditable A I on a sensing network that is already deployed.

167
00:08:40,731 --> 00:08:42,778
That is the opportunity:

168
00:08:42,778 --> 00:08:47,531
protecting substations, ports and airports without new radar.

169
00:08:47,531 --> 00:08:52,064
Try it at ai-agentic-enterprises dot com slash six-g-isac.

170
00:08:52,064 --> 00:08:58,060
I am Yuvaraj Aravindan, building governed A I agents for regulated industries.
